        <title type="html"><![CDATA[Add backdrop-filter: url()]]></title>
        <id>https://github.com/Fyrd/caniuse/issues/7354</id>
        <link href="https://github.com/Fyrd/caniuse/issues/7354"/>
        <updated>2025-08-14T05:34:23.000Z</updated>
        <content type="html"><![CDATA[<img src="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/515525?u=266b38ed1b35fecf38b76beec74bb4a296715c68&v=4" width="64" height="64" alt=""/><br/><p dir="auto">All of these fancy iOS26 liquid glass demos rely on backdrop-filter: url(). See: <a href="https://frontendmasters.com/blog/liquid-glass-on-the-web/" rel="nofollow">https://frontendmasters.com/blog/liquid-glass-on-the-web/</a></p>
<p dir="auto">It's not supported on <a href="https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=245510" rel="nofollow">Safari</a> or <a href="https://connect.mozilla.org/t5/ideas/support-svg-filters-in-backdrop-filter-for-advanced-glass/idi-p/98453" rel="nofollow">Firefox</a>.</p>
<p dir="auto">A search for "Backdrop Filter" brings up a listing for <a href="https://caniuse.com/css-backdrop-filter" rel="nofollow">the basic effects</a>, but that's not helpful when figuring out what these liquid glass demos will run on.</p>
<p dir="auto">Thank you for this wonderful resource.</p>]]></content>
